## Verdict

SOLVED: no

The conjectured “if and only if” with the uniform conditional tail condition is false. The tail condition is a clean sufficient condition, but it is not necessary. A sharper truncation-based sufficient condition is available, and there are concrete counterexamples showing why a full necessity theorem needs an information/learnability condition, not just a raw hitting-delay tail condition.

## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt

Use the comparator value, not the worst conditional delay tail, as the main object. Define

\[
V_T(a)=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a),\qquad
V^H_T(a)=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a),
\]

where

\[
r^H_t(a)=a\mathbf 1\{\tau_t(a)\le \min(H,T-t)\}.
\]

Use sup rather than max unless atomlessness or upper-semicontinuity is assumed:

\[
V_T^\star=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}V_T(a),\qquad
V_{T,H}^\star=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}V_T^H(a).
\]

Define the horizon-wise comparator truncation loss

\[
B_T(H)=V_T^\star-V_{T,H}^\star.
\]

Then delayed Hedge on a grid \(\mathcal G_K=\{0,1/K,\dots,(K-1)/K\}\) gives the candidate upper bound

\[
R(T)
\;\le\;
C\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}
+\frac{T}{K}
+B_T(H).
\]

Thus sublinear regret follows if there are \(H_T,K_T\) such that

\[
H_T\log K_T=o(T),\qquad T/K_T=o(T),\qquad B_T(H_T)=o(T).
\]

With \(K=T\), this becomes

\[
R(T)\lesssim \sqrt{T(H+1)\log T}+B_T(H)+1.
\]

The original \(\beta\)-condition implies this because

\[
B_T(H)
\le
\sup_a \mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T
a\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}
\le T\beta(H).
\]

So the LaTeX upper bound is valid, but it uses a stronger-than-needed tail assumption.

If \(\beta(h)\lesssim h^{-\alpha}\), taking \(K=T\) and balancing gives

\[
H(T)\asymp (T/\log T)^{1/(2\alpha+1)}
\]

and

\[
R(T)\lesssim
T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}
(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}.
\]

If \(\beta\) decays exponentially, \(H(T)\asymp \log T\) gives roughly \(\sqrt T\) regret up to logarithms.

## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions

1. Comparator-side exact reduction:

Let

\[
M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad
M_{t,H}=\max_{t<s\le \min(t+H,T)}P_s.
\]

Then

\[
V_T(a)=a\sum_{t=1}^T\Pr(M_t>a),
\qquad
V_T^H(a)=a\sum_{t=1}^T\Pr(M_{t,H}>a).
\]

So \(B_T(H)\) measures whether the best fixed quote’s value is preserved by looking only \(H\) steps ahead.

2. Truncation theorem:

For any learner,

\[
R_T
\le
B_T(H)
+
\left[
V_{T,H}^\star-\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t^H(a_t)
\right].
\]

The second bracket is just regret in the bounded-delay truncated game.

3. Grid discretization:

For the grid \(\mathcal G_K\),

\[
V_{T,H}^\star
\le
\sup_{b\in\mathcal G_K}V_T^H(b)+T/K.
\]

This is the rounding-down argument already in `delays.tex`.

4. Delayed full-information learning:

For \(K\) grid arms and feedback delay at most \(H\),

\[
\sup_{b\in\mathcal G_K}V_T^H(b)
-
\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t^H(a_t)
\le
C\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}.
\]

This is the standard delayed expert-advice bound.

## Gaps And Failure Points

The condition \(B_T(H_T)=o(T)\) is only a sufficient condition, not a full characterization. It says the optimal comparator’s value is not mostly hidden beyond a sublinear delay window. But a learner can sometimes exploit long-delayed rewards if the good quote is predictable from observed prices.

The current regret definition uses \(\max_a\). With strict trade condition \(P>a\), deterministic atomic prices can make the maximum fail to exist. The clean formulation should use \(\sup_a\), or impose atomlessness/regularity.

A true iff theorem needs a model choice: exogenous stochastic process, adaptive adversary, horizon-dependent process, whether the learner knows \(T\), and whether guarantees are process-specific or uniform over a class.

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

Uniform \(\beta\) is not necessary.

Let \(t_n=2^n\), set \(P_{t_n}=U_n/n\) with \(U_n\sim \mathrm{Unif}[1,2]\), and set \(P_t=0\) otherwise. Then for \(a=0\), after each peak the next positive price is an arbitrarily long deterministic time away, so

\[
\beta(h)=1
\]

for every \(h\). But for \(T\in[2^n,2^{n+1})\), the best fixed quote value is at most \(O(2^n/n)=o(T)\). Since all learner rewards are nonnegative, every learner has regret at most \(V_T^\star=o(T)\). Thus sublinear regret is possible while the stated tail condition fails maximally.

There is also an opposite obstruction: long delays can be fatal when the delayed event hides the relevant quote. For horizon \(T\), take two paths identical until the last round:

\[
P_1=\cdots=P_{T-1}=0,\qquad P_T\in\{p_L,p_H\},
\]

with \(0<p_L<p_H<1\). Before time \(T\), the learner cannot distinguish the two environments. For one of them, any learner suffers \(\Omega(T)\) regret against the best fixed quote. With \(p_L=1/3,p_H=2/3\), the lower-bound constant is at least \(1/6\) per active round, up to the usual \(\epsilon\) issue from strict inequality.

So the right necessity statement cannot be “long tails bad” in isolation; it must say that long-delayed value is both significant and not inferable from earlier observations.

## Promising Ideas To Explore

A plausible paper theorem is:

\[
R(T)\lesssim
\inf_{H,K}
\left\{
\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}
+T/K
+B_T(H)
\right\}.
\]

Then present \(\beta\) as an easy checkable sufficient condition via \(B_T(H)\le T\beta(H)\).

For necessity, pursue indistinguishable-prefix lower bounds: construct pairs or families of price processes with identical observed histories up to time \(T-H\), different optimal quotes, and large comparator value arriving only after the indistinguishable region.

A stronger characterization may need to decompose regret into “truncatable comparator value” plus “predictable long-delay value.”

## Notes For Critics

Check the delayed-expert reduction under the exact feedback model: observing \(P_t\) reveals all grid arms whose outstanding quotes would have traded, and untraded quotes can be closed after \(H\) steps in the truncated game.

The counterexample against \(\beta\)-necessity is robust because the failure occurs at \(a=0\), but the attainable comparator value is \(o(T)\). This directly attacks the essential supremum over all actions/histories.

The main unresolved issue is a genuine iff theorem. The current best candidate is a sharp sufficient theorem plus lower-bound templates showing what an additional information condition must rule out.
